  "_legend": "Tideglass is a harbour city of thirty-one thousand, white stone under blue pantile, laid on a grid that gave up when it reached the water. Gate Avenue comes in from the north road and runs down to the Tide Hall, which stands across the whole head of its own square with the Basin -- a hand's depth of harbour water laid in stone -- in front of it. The Glassrun, a canal cut from the north wall to the harbour, splits the city in two and is crossed at four bridges; the Cut branches west through the old town and is crossed at three more. West of the Glassrun, north to south: the Residential District of brick tenements, the Kin Hall District, the Commercial District along the Parade, and the Old Harbour on the water. East of it: the Ocean Research District with the Meridian Foundation Coastal Station in it, more stores along the Parade, and the Dockyards. Two moles reach out into the bay -- the west one carries the Lighthouse District, the east one the deep-water slips.",
  "_plan": "A hundred and nineteen roofs and twenty-seven doors, and the ratio is the point. Stonewake proved that scale is carried by buildings that never open, and a city three times its size has to look three times denser without becoming three times as many rooms to furnish. Each district is built from a different frontage kit -- tenement brick and slate up north, white stone and blue pantile on the civic and commercial streets, timber and mossed hip in the old town, plaster and terracotta sheds in the yards, glazed flat-roofed blocks in the research quarter -- so a player always knows which part of the city they are standing in without reading a sign. Two rules were learned the hard way and hold everywhere here: a crossing over water is never one tile wide, because the feet box is eleven pixels on a sixteen pixel tile and anyone who steps onto a single-tile bridge off-centre stops dead in the canal; and nothing solid, ornament or townsperson, stands where it would pinch a through route, which is checked by flooding the map with every one of them standing at once. The canals are the map's second job: impassable until the Tide Hall teaches Wade, so the Glassrun, the Cut, the Basin and the wet dock are all reasons to come back, and three of the eleven pickups sit in them."
}
